# Normalize line endings, for the same reason the Loom does: this tree is edited on Windows and
# built under WSL, and a CRLF working tree makes shell scripts fail under WSL bash. `text=auto`
# lets git treat files as text and normalize on commit; `eol=lf` forces LF on checkout whatever
# a user's core.autocrlf says.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fonts are BINARY. `text=auto eol=lf` above would otherwise let git normalize line
# endings inside a TTF -- silently, on checkout, on a Windows working tree -- and the
# corruption's only symptom would be a font that fails to open at runtime. The OS window and the
# SDL skin embed `graphics/window/fonts/JetBrainsMono-Regular.ttf` and
# `surface/fonts/JetBrainsMono-Regular.ttf`, so their bytes have to be the bytes upstream
# published.
*.ttf -text
*.otf -text

# Toolbox files are serialized typed values, bytes a line-ending conversion would corrupt.
*.toolbox -text

# An app's view and flow are Loom's canonical binary, as the View Builder and Flow save them.
*.view -text
*.flow -text
